## Objective

A fully integrated, decision-ready protocol translating current exercise physiology, sports nutrition, recovery science, and applied neuroscience into daily practice. Every recommendation is a strategic step performance staff can execute immediately — aimed at maximizing match performance, accelerating multi-timescale recovery, reducing non-contact injury risk, and sustaining player availability across a season.

---

## PART I — 48–24 Hours Before the Match: Glycogen Supercompensation & System Priming

**Sleep**
- Protect 8–10 hours of sleep opportunity, bedtime ±30 min
- Room at 17–19°C, full darkness, no screens in the final 45–60 min
- *Evidence:* Sleep extension to 10h improves sprint times and shooting accuracy (Mah et al., 2011); sleep under 6h raises injury risk up to 70% in adolescent athletes (Milewski et al., 2014)

**Hydration**
- Target urine colour ≤3 on the Armstrong scale all day
- Hot/heavy sessions: add 500–1000 mg sodium per litre
- *Evidence:* Pre-exercise hypohydration >2% body mass degrades high-intensity intermittent performance (McGregor et al., 1999)

**Nutrition — Carbohydrate Loading**

*Step 1 — Loading duration*
| Day | Target |
|---|---|
| MD-4, MD-3 | 7–10 g/kg/day, taper training volume |
| MD-2 | 6–8 g/kg |
| MD-1 | 6–8 g/kg, low-fibre, familiar foods |

*Evidence:* A 3-day high-carb taper can raise muscle glycogen ~50–100% above baseline (Bussau et al., 2002; Burke et al., 2011); single-day loading only partially restores heavily depleted stores.

*Step 2 — Source selection*
- From MD-2: low-residue carbs — white rice, pasta, skinless white potato, sourdough, cereal, banana, sports drinks/gels
- Watermelon, cantaloupe, honeydew for fluid + glucose support
- Limit high-fructose fruit loads (excess apples/pears) to avoid gut distress

*Step 3 — Protein pacing*
- 2.0–2.5 g/kg/day (~150 g for a 75 kg player), in 4–6 servings of ~30 g every 3–4 hours
- Example: 08:00 / 12:00 / 15:00 / 19:00 / 22:00 (casein-rich)
- *Evidence:* This pattern maximizes 24h muscle protein synthesis (Areta et al., 2013)

*Step 4 — Fat and fibre*
- Fat ~1 g/kg/day in the final 48h; fibre under 15–20 g on MD-1
- Skinless chicken/turkey, white fish, egg whites, low-fat Greek yogurt, refined grains

*Step 5 — Micronutrients*
- 1 cup bright vegetables at lunch and dinner (spinach, peppers, beetroot) for nitrate/antioxidant support
- Daily berries or citrus — vitamin C and polyphenols, may temper excess inflammation without blunting adaptation (Nieman et al., 2012)

**Avoid:** alcohol, high-fat meals, heavily processed/untested foods

