## PART III — During the Match: Fuel & Hydrate

- High-intensity play (>70% peak HR): CHO oxidation up to 2–3 g/min
- 30 g carbs/hour via 6–7% sports drink or gel + water
- Hot/humid (>25°C WBGT): electrolyte drink (Na 460–1150 mg/L) over plain water
- **Halftime:** 150–300 ml carb-electrolyte drink (~15g CHO), tactical talk in shade/cool
- *Evidence:* Mouth-rinsing activates reward centres but ingestion is needed for sustained performance (Carter et al., 2004); 6% glucose optimizes gastric emptying (Jeukendrup, 2011); sodium aids fluid retention (Maughan & Shirreffs, 2008)

---

## PART IV — Immediately After the Match (0–30 Min): "The Golden Hour"

**Active Recovery**
- 5–10 min light jog/bike/walk with nasal breathing (4s in, 6s out) for parasympathetic reactivation
- *Evidence:* Speeds lactate clearance, reduces stiffness — intensity must stay below first ventilatory threshold (Menzies et al., 2010)

**Two-Phase Refuelling**

*Phase 1 (0–30 min):* 20–40g whey protein + 1.0–1.2 g/kg CHO (75–90g for 75kg player)
- Options: whey shake (25g protein + 80g maltodextrin/dextrose); 500ml chocolate milk + banana; Greek yogurt + honey + rice cakes

*Phase 2 (2–3 hours):* Large meal bringing total to 1.0–1.5 g/kg CHO/hour over 4h (300–450g total for 75kg player, ~220–370g remaining after the shake)
- Example: 300g rice, 200g sweet potato, 150g salmon, 100g broccoli, fruit salad
- *Evidence:* 1.0–1.2 g/kg/h in hour one maximizes the insulin-sensitive resynthesis window (Ivy et al., 2002; Jentjens & Jeukendrup, 2003); a large meal at 2–3h works as well as hourly snacks (Burke et al., 2004); added protein aids storage when CHO intake is suboptimal (Betts et al., 2010)

**Rehydration — Replace 150% of Losses**
- Weigh pre/post match (1kg loss ≈ 1L sweat)
- Provide 150% of the deficit as electrolyte fluid (50–80 mmol/L Na) over 2–4h
- *Evidence:* Plain water suppresses thirst and increases urine output before full rehydration; sodium restores plasma volume (Shirreffs et al., 1996)

---

## PART V — First 6 Hours: Extended Recovery Window

**Meal structure:** 50% high-GI carbs / 25% lean protein / 25% vegetables + healthy fats
- Examples: chicken + rice + roasted vegetables; salmon + quinoa + courgettes; pasta + lean beef ragu
- Glycogen may take 24h+ to fully restore after exhaustive intermittent exercise (Nicholas et al., 1999)

**Recovery techniques**
- Foam rolling 15–20 min (quads, hamstrings, glutes, adductors) — reduces DOMS perception (Pearcey et al., 2015)
- Compression garments 3–4h — may reduce soreness, speed strength recovery (Hill et al., 2014)
- 10 min gentle mobility

**Cold-Water Immersion — a decision, not a default**
- Recent meta-analyses/trials show CWI doesn't outperform placebo/active recovery for strength, sprint, or biomarker recovery beyond a small perceptual effect (Poppendieck et al., 2013; Higgins et al., 2023)
- Use only if: player reports strong perceptual benefit, primary goal is heat dissipation, or short-term analgesia is needed
- Not mandatory — flagged "optional/low-priority" in the AI engine
- If used: 10–15 min at 10–15°C, then a warm shower to avoid blunting adaptation via prolonged vasoconstriction (Roberts et al., 2015)

---

## PART VI — Evening Recovery: Consolidate & Repair

- 8–10h sleep, dark, cool (17–19°C)
- Optional 20-min nap before 16:00
- 30-min pre-bed "digital sunset" — no screens, reading or breathing exercises only

**Overnight nutrition**
- 30–40g casein 30–45 min before sleep (cottage cheese, casein shake, Greek yogurt + almonds)
- *Evidence:* Pre-sleep casein raises overnight muscle protein synthesis ~22% without impairing sleep (Res et al., 2012; Trommelen et al., 2017)

---

## PART VII — Recovery Day (24h Post-Match): Rebuild, Don't Overload

- Resting metabolic rate can stay elevated 5–10% for up to 48h post-match (Koehler et al., 2016) — plan a deliberate caloric surplus vs. a normal rest day
- Carbs: 5 g/kg if >10km with high-speed actions logged; 3 g/kg if a substitute
- Protein: maintain 2 g/kg
- Example day: oatmeal + whey + berries + almonds → egg omelette + sweet potato + spinach → salmon/beef + quinoa + vegetables
- Active recovery: 30–45 min non-impact (swim, bike ≤50% peak power, yoga, mobility)
- No heavy resistance/speed work unless HRV and CMJ data return to baseline

