## PART IX — AI Recovery Integration: The Recuperating Engine (RE)

The RE computes a daily **Recovery Score (0–100)**, fusing external load, internal load, and wellness data via a squad-trained model, then issues just-in-time recommendations.

**Inputs**
- External: GPS/optical — total distance, sprint distance (>24 km/h), accel/decel >2 m/s², dynamic stress load
- Internal: session-RPE (duration × RPE), mean/max HR, HRV, HR recovery
- Wellness: sleep quality, hydration, nutrition compliance, soreness, psychological readiness, injury history

**Injury Prediction — with caveats**
- ML models fusing GPS + RPE have shown retrospective AUC ~0.83 for non-contact injury prediction in some cohorts (Rossi et al., 2018) — but prospective real-world accuracy typically drops to 60–70%
- Treat "high risk" flags as decision support, not verdicts: a red flag triggers a mandatory staff huddle before any load change
- Real Sociedad reported flagging over 33% of injuries in advance using an integrated model, reducing incidence via load management (Lago-Peñas et al., 2020) — realistic with ensemble models continuously retrained on club-specific data

**Daily outputs (staff-validated, not auto-applied)**
- Hydration volume — from sweat rate, temperature, session duration
- Carbohydrate prescription — periodized g/kg with meal-timing windows
- Protein targets — distributed pulses, pre-sleep casein flag if soreness/damage markers are high
- Sleep target — extended window or nap suggestion if sleep debt >1h
- Recovery modalities — foam rolling, contrast therapy; CWI only if player preference exists
- Training load modification — % reduction in sprint volume/duration if Recovery Score <65
- Injury-risk colour code: green (baseline) / amber (caution, −20% load) / red (do not train, investigate)

The model self-adjusts thresholds using feedback loops — e.g., if a load reduction produces an HRV rebound and low soreness, it recalibrates.

